# Gefen EXT-UHD600A Matrix Switcher Driver
#
# Controls Gefen EXT-UHD600A-44 (4x4) and EXT-UHD600A-88 (8x8) 4K Ultra HD
# 600 MHz HDMI matrix switchers with HDR and analog audio de-embedding,
# over Telnet (port 23) or RS-232 (19200 8N1).
#
# Protocol references (manufacturer user manuals; Gefen is a Nice North
# America brand, ex Nortek / Core Brands — the manuals carry the full
# RS-232 / Telnet / UDP command chapters):
#   44: https://www.markertek.com/Attachments/Manuals/Gefen/EXT-UHD600A-44-Manual.pdf
#       (Version A2, 192 pages; also on resources.nortekcontrol.com)
#   88: https://www.egeratedocuments.com/markalar/gefen/Manual/EXT-UHD600A-88-Manual.pdf
#       (Version A1, 232 pages; also on resources.nortekcontrol.com)
# Both manuals document the same CLI grammar; the models differ only in
# frame width and panel hardware (44: per-output video scaling and
# button/LED brightness; 88: LCD display brightness, HTTP-port and
# Telnet-welcome settings).
#
# Wire format:
#   Plain ASCII lines. "#"-prefixed get/set commands ("#get_vol a",
#   "#set_mute a 1") plus bare routing verbs: "r <input> <output...>"
#   (outputs are LETTERS A-H, input X = off, output 0 = all), "s" (full
#   routing dump "S A 1 B 2 C 3 D X") and "p <n>" (preset recall).
#   Replies restate state in uppercase ("VOL A 100", "MUTE A 0",
#   "R A 1 B 1 C 1"). Commands are case-insensitive; this driver sends
#   CRLF-terminated lines and frames replies on CRLF.
#
# Push vs poll: POLLED, with opt-in push. The device has a persisted
# "RS-232 Feedback" setting (#set_feedback, factory default on) that
# emits unsolicited state feedback — the manual scopes it to the RS-232
# port ("Main RS-232 Feedback"), and UDP has its own remote-feedback
# channel; nothing documents unsolicited frames on Telnet sessions. The
# setting is exposed here as the "RS-232 Feedback" device setting rather
# than force-armed on connect (arming it from a Telnet session would
# flip a persisted setting that affects whatever sits on the serial
# port). The response rules double as push handlers, so any unsolicited
# feedback that does arrive — RS-232 connections in particular — updates
# state immediately; polling is the steady-state resync either way.
#
# Reply-grammar quirks handled here (all from the manuals):
#   - #get_input_hdcp (configured handshake mode 0/1/2) and
#     #gets_input_hdcp (live status 1/2/U/F) both answer
#     "INPUT_HDCP <in> <v>" with overlapping value sets. Only the live
#     status is polled; a #set_input_hdcp echo briefly writes its mode
#     digit into the status until the next poll refreshes it. Same
#     collision on OUTPUT_HDCP (mode 0/1 vs status 1/2/U/F).
#   - Both manuals' #gets_input_hpd examples answer "INPUT_HDCP <in> <v>"
#     (a doc typo, or the firmware reuses the token) — the HPD rule
#     accepts either token with an L/H value, disjoint from the HDCP
#     value sets.
#   - "0"-target set commands ("#set_mute 0 1") answer multi-pair lines
#     whose exact shape the manuals don't show; those echoes go unparsed
#     and the next poll refreshes state. Routing multi-pair echoes ARE
#     documented ("R A 1 B 1 C 1") and parse at any width.
#   - The Telnet welcome banner and "telnet->" prompt arrive without
#     their own line ending, so response patterns tolerate a leading
#     prompt fragment (no start anchor; tight token boundaries).
#
# Telnet login: the device can require a password at the start of a
# Telnet session ("Require Password on Connect", factory default OFF;
# sessions are fixed to the Admin user). Neither manual documents the
# literal login prompt strings, so no auth block is declared — the
# driver works with the factory default. If login is enabled on the
# matrix, disable it (web UI: Network > Telnet Settings) for driver use.
#
# Why YAML: strictly line-oriented request/response ASCII where every
# reply restates identity + value — declarative rosters (letter output
# ids via ids_from, integer input count), child_set fan-out (optional
# capture pairs cover every routing-echo width), and device_settings
# cover the whole documented surface. Modeled on wyrestorm_mx.

id: gefen_uhd600a
name: Gefen EXT-UHD600A Matrix
manufacturer: Gefen
category: switcher
version: 1.1.0
author: OpenAVC
min_platform_version: "0.27.0"
description: >
  Controls Gefen EXT-UHD600A-44 and EXT-UHD600A-88 4K HDMI matrix
  switchers via Telnet on port 23 or RS-232. Every output is a child
  entity tracking its routed input, analog audio volume/mute, masking,
  sink status, and HDCP; every input is a child tracking signal
  presence, HDCP, and EDID mode. Covers routing presets, input/output
  naming, HDCP modes, per-output video scaling (44), panel brightness,
  IR channel, and the RS-232 feedback setting.

source_url: https://www.markertek.com/Attachments/Manuals/Gefen/EXT-UHD600A-44-Manual.pdf
tags: [matrix-switcher, hdmi, 4k, hdr, gefen, telnet]
verified: false
simulated: true
protocols: [gefen_cli]
ports: [23]
compatible_models:
  - manufacturer: Gefen
    models:
      - EXT-UHD600A-44
      - EXT-UHD600A-88
    confidence: untested
    notes: >
      One CLI grammar covers both frames. For the 44 keep the default
      Output IDs "A,B,C,D" and Input Count 4; for the 88 set
      "A,B,C,D,E,F,G,H" and 8. Per-output video scaling and the
      button/LED brightness settings exist on the 44 only; the LCD
      brightness setting exists on the 88 only — the other model answers
      an error the driver ignores, and unsupported settings simply never
      populate. If "Require Password on Connect" is enabled for Telnet,
      disable it (Network > Telnet Settings) for driver use.

transport: tcp
transports: [tcp, serial]
delimiter: "\r\n"

help:
  overview: >
    Gefen EXT-UHD600A matrices route up to eight 4K HDMI inputs to up to
    eight outputs, with analog audio de-embedding per output. Each
    output is a child entity showing its routed input (0 = off), analog
    volume and mute, video masking, sink hot-plug/Rsense status, and
    HDCP; each input is a child showing signal presence, HDCP status,
    and EDID mode. Sixteen routing presets can be recalled and renamed.
    Front-panel names given to inputs and outputs label the children.
  setup: >
    1. Add the matrix by IP with Telnet port 23 (factory default), or
    pick Direct serial / a bridge for RS-232 at 19200 8N1.
    2. Match the roster to the frame: the defaults describe a 44
    (Output IDs "A,B,C,D", Input Count 4); for an 88 set
    "A,B,C,D,E,F,G,H" and 8. Use uppercase letters.
    3. If the matrix has "Require Password on Connect" enabled for
    Telnet (off from the factory), disable it in the web UI (Network >
    Telnet Settings) — the login prompt is not machine-documented.
    4. For live push updates over RS-232, enable the RS-232 Feedback
    device setting (on from the factory); polling covers Telnet.

discovery:
  # Banner-read probe: the Telnet CLI greets server-first with
  # "Welcome to EXT-UHD600A-44 Telnet" (manual, Telnet Configuration).
  # Connect-only — no bytes are sent on the shared Telnet port. If the
  # welcome message is hidden (a web-UI setting), the probe simply
  # never matches and the alias hints remain.
  tcp_probe:
    port: 23
    expect_regex: 'Welcome to EXT-UHD600A'
    extract_manufacturer: "Gefen"
    extract:
      model: { regex: 'Welcome to (EXT-UHD600A-\d+)', group: 1 }
  manufacturer_alias:
    - "gefen"

default_config:
  host: ""
  port: 23
  baudrate: 19200
  output_ids: "A,B,C,D"
  input_count: 4
  poll_interval: 20
  inter_command_delay: 0.05

config_schema:
  host:
    type: string
    required: true
    label: IP Address
  port:
    type: integer
    default: 23
    label: Telnet Port
    description: "EXT-UHD600A matrices listen on Telnet port 23 by default."
  baudrate:
    type: integer
    default: 19200
    label: Baud Rate (serial)
    description: "RS-232 control runs at 19200 8N1, no flow control."
  output_ids:
    type: string
    default: "A,B,C,D"
    label: Output IDs
    description: >
      Comma-separated output letters, uppercase — each becomes an Output
      child. "A,B,C,D" for the 44, "A,B,C,D,E,F,G,H" for the 88.
  input_count:
    type: integer
    default: 4
    min: 0
    max: 8
    label: Input Count
    description: >
      Number of HDMI inputs — each becomes an Input child. 4 for the
      44, 8 for the 88.
  poll_interval:
    type: integer
    default: 20
    min: 0
    label: Poll Interval (sec)
    description: >
      Refreshes routing plus per-output volume, mute, masking, audio
      mode, sink status, HDCP, scaling, and name, and per-input signal,
      HPD, mode, HDCP, EDID mode, and name. Set to 0 to disable.
  inter_command_delay:
    type: number
    default: 0.05
    min: 0
    label: Inter-Command Delay (sec)
    description: "Brief safety delay between commands (none documented by Gefen)."

state_variables:
  firmware_version:
    type: string
    label: Firmware Version
    help: "Matrix firmware version, read on connect."
  device_desc:
    type: string
    label: Device Description
    help: "The matrix's device-description string (also shown by the Syner-G discovery tool)."
  rs232_feedback:
    type: boolean
    label: RS-232 Feedback
    help: >
      True when the matrix emits unsolicited state feedback on its
      RS-232 port (factory default on). With feedback on and the driver
      connected over RS-232, front-panel and web changes update state
      instantly instead of at the next poll.
  ir_channel:
    type: enum
    values: ["1", "2", "3", "4"]
    label: IR Channel
    help: "IR channel the matrix listens on — must match the IR remote's channel switch."
  led_brightness:
    type: integer
    label: LED Brightness
    min: 1
    max: 100
    help: "Front-panel LED indicator brightness (EXT-UHD600A-44 only)."
  button_brightness:
    type: integer
    label: Button Brightness
    min: 1
    max: 100
    help: "Front-panel button brightness (EXT-UHD600A-44 only)."
  lcd_brightness:
    type: integer
    label: LCD Brightness
    min: 0
    max: 100
    help: "Front-panel LCD display brightness (EXT-UHD600A-88 only)."

# One video plane. The outputs are string-keyed, which is why a destination
# carries whatever the device calls it rather than a row number.
routing:
  destination_child_type: output
  source_child_type: input
  command: route
  planes:
    - label: Video
      route_property: input

child_entity_types:
  input:
    label: Input
    label_plural: Inputs
    id_format: { type: integer, min: 1, max: 8 }
    label_field: name
    state_variables:
      signal:
        type: boolean
        label: Signal Present
        help: "True when a valid clock signal is detected at this HDMI input."
        cloud_priority: high
      hpd:
        type: string
        label: HPD State
        help: "Hot-plug detect state toward the source: high or low."
      video_mode:
        type: string
        label: Video Mode
        help: "Signal type detected at this input: hdmi or dvi."
      hdcp_status:
        type: string
        label: HDCP Status
        help: >
          Live HDCP status: 1 = encrypted HDCP 1.4, 2 = encrypted HDCP
          2.2, U = unencrypted, F = fail. Briefly shows the configured
          handshake mode digit right after Set Input HDCP Mode (the
          device reuses the same reply token); the next poll restores
          the live status.
      edid_mode:
        type: integer
        label: EDID Mode
        min: 0
        max: 7
        help: >
          EDID source for this input: 0-5 = internal presets (720p/
          1080p/4K, 2ch/multichannel), 6 = external (downstream sink),
          7 = custom.
      name:
        type: string
        label: Name
        help: "Input name stored in the matrix (up to 30 characters); labels this child."
    summary_fields: [signal, hdcp_status]
    instances:
      count_from: input_count
      label: "Input {id}"
  output:
    label: Output
    label_plural: Outputs
    id_format: { type: string }
    label_field: name
    state_variables:
      input:
        type: integer
        label: Routed Input
        min: 0
        max: 8
        control: true
        cloud_priority: high
        help: "Input routed to this output (0 = off / no input)."
      volume:
        type: integer
        label: Volume
        min: 0
        max: 100
        step: 1
        control: true
        help: "Analog audio de-embed volume for this output (0-100)."
      mute:
        type: boolean
        label: Mute
        control: true
        cloud_priority: high
        help: "True when this output's analog audio is muted."
      mask:
        type: boolean
        label: Mask
        control: true
        cloud_priority: high
        help: "True when this output's video is masked (blanked)."
      audio_mode:
        type: string
        label: Audio Mode
        help: "Analog audio mode: fixed or variable (variable follows the volume setting)."
      hpd:
        type: string
        label: HPD State
        help: "Hot-plug detect state from the connected display: high or low."
      rsense:
        type: string
        label: Rsense
        help: "Receiver sense: high when the attached display is powered (termination detected)."
      video_mode:
        type: string
        label: Video Mode
        help: "Signal type on this output: hdmi or dvi."
      hdcp_status:
        type: string
        label: HDCP Status
        help: >
          Live HDCP status: 1 = encrypted HDCP 1.4, 2 = encrypted HDCP
          2.2, U = unencrypted, F = fail. Briefly shows the configured
          mode digit (0 = follow input, 1 = always encode) right after
          Set Output HDCP Mode; the next poll restores the live status.
      scaling:
        type: boolean
        label: Video Scaling
        help: >
          EXT-UHD600A-44 only — true when scaling is enabled on this
          output (A and C downscale 2160p to 1080p; B and D upscale
          1080p to 2160p).
      name:
        type: string
        label: Name
        help: "Output name stored in the matrix (up to 30 characters); labels this child."
    summary_fields: [input, mute]
    instances:
      ids_from: output_ids
      label: "Output {id}"

# Persisted device-side values with documented read-back. Per-output /
# per-input values live on the children instead (settled rule); preset
# names have a per-slot dimension a flat setting can't take, so they
# stay on the Set Preset Name command.
device_settings:
  rs232_feedback:
    type: boolean
    label: RS-232 Feedback
    help: >
      Emit unsolicited state feedback on the RS-232 port (factory
      default on). Enable for live push updates when controlling over
      RS-232; the setting is persisted on the matrix and shared with
      anything else on that port.
    state_key: rs232_feedback
    default: true
    setup: false
    write:
      send: "#set_feedback {value:d}\r\n"
  ir_channel:
    type: enum
    values: ["1", "2", "3", "4"]
    label: IR Channel
    help: "IR channel the matrix listens on — set it to match the IR remote."
    state_key: ir_channel
    default: "1"
    setup: false
    write:
      send: "#set_ir_channel {value}\r\n"
  device_desc:
    type: string
    label: Device Description
    help: "Description string shown by the Syner-G discovery tool."
    state_key: device_desc
    default: ""
    setup: false
    write:
      send: "#set_device_desc {value}\r\n"
  led_brightness:
    type: integer
    label: LED Brightness
    min: 1
    max: 100
    help: "Front-panel LED indicator brightness, 1-100 (EXT-UHD600A-44 only)."
    state_key: led_brightness
    default: 60
    setup: false
    write:
      send: "#set_led_brightness {value}\r\n"
  button_brightness:
    type: integer
    label: Button Brightness
    min: 1
    max: 100
    help: "Front-panel button brightness, 1-100 (EXT-UHD600A-44 only)."
    state_key: button_brightness
    default: 60
    setup: false
    write:
      send: "#set_button_brightness {value}\r\n"
  lcd_brightness:
    type: integer
    label: LCD Brightness
    min: 0
    max: 100
    help: "Front-panel LCD brightness, 0-100 (EXT-UHD600A-88 only)."
    state_key: lcd_brightness
    default: 75
    setup: false
    write:
      send: "#set_lcd_brightness {value}\r\n"

commands:

  # ── Routing ──

  route:
    label: Route Input to Output
    send: "r {input} {output}\r\n"
    help: "Route a video input to an output."
    params:
      input:
        type: child_id
        child_type: input
        required: true
        label: Input
      output:
        type: child_id
        child_type: output
        required: true
        label: Output

  route_all:
    label: Route Input to All Outputs
    send: "r {input} 0\r\n"
    params:
      input:
        type: child_id
        child_type: input
        required: true
        label: Input

  route_off:
    label: Turn Off Output
    send: "r x {output}\r\n"
    help: "Route 'no input' to an output — the output behaves as if no source is connected."
    params:
      output:
        type: child_id
        child_type: output
        required: true
        label: Output

  recall_preset:
    label: Recall Routing Preset
    send: "p {preset}\r\n"
    help: "Recall one of the 16 routing presets (presets are saved from the web interface or front panel)."
    params:
      preset:
        type: integer
        required: true
        label: Preset
        min: 1
        max: 16

  mask_on:
    label: Mask Output
    send: "#set_mask {output} 1\r\n"
    help: "Mask (blank) an output's video."
    params:
      output:
        type: child_id
        child_type: output
        required: true
        label: Output

  mask_off:
    label: Unmask Output
    send: "#set_mask {output} 0\r\n"
    params:
      output:
        type: child_id
        child_type: output
        required: true
        label: Output

  # ── Analog audio ──

  set_volume:
    label: Set Output Volume
    send: "#set_vol {output} {level}\r\n"
    help: "Set the analog audio de-embed volume for an output."
    params:
      output:
        type: child_id
        child_type: output
        required: true
        label: Output
      level:
        type: integer
        required: true
        label: Volume
        min: 0
        max: 100

  mute_on:
    label: Mute Output Audio
    send: "#set_mute {output} 1\r\n"
    params:
      output:
        type: child_id
        child_type: output
        required: true
        label: Output

  mute_off:
    label: Unmute Output Audio
    send: "#set_mute {output} 0\r\n"
    params:
      output:
        type: child_id
        child_type: output
        required: true
        label: Output

  mute_all_on:
    label: Mute All Outputs
    send: "#set_mute 0 1\r\n"
    help: "Mute the analog audio on every output (state refreshes at the next poll)."

  mute_all_off:
    label: Unmute All Outputs
    send: "#set_mute 0 0\r\n"

  set_audio_mode:
    label: Set Output Audio Mode
    send: "#set_audio_output {output} {mode}\r\n"
    help: "Fixed = line level; variable = follows the volume setting."
    params:
      output:
        type: child_id
        child_type: output
        required: true
        label: Output
      mode:
        type: enum
        required: true
        label: Mode
        values:
          - { value: "f", label: "Fixed" }
          - { value: "v", label: "Variable" }

  # ── Names ──

  set_input_name:
    label: Set Input Name
    send: "#set_io_name {input} {name}\r\n"
    help: "Name an input (up to 30 characters); the name labels the Input child."
    params:
      input:
        type: child_id
        child_type: input
        required: true
        label: Input
      name:
        type: string
        required: true
        label: Name
        pattern: "^.{1,30}$"

  set_output_name:
    label: Set Output Name
    send: "#set_io_name {output} {name}\r\n"
    params:
      output:
        type: child_id
        child_type: output
        required: true
        label: Output
      name:
        type: string
        required: true
        label: Name
        pattern: "^.{1,30}$"

  set_preset_name:
    label: Set Preset Name
    send: "#set_preset_name {preset} {name}\r\n"
    params:
      preset:
        type: integer
        required: true
        label: Preset
        min: 1
        max: 16
      name:
        type: string
        required: true
        label: Name
        pattern: "^.{1,30}$"

  # ── HDCP / EDID / signal ──

  set_input_hdcp:
    label: Set Input HDCP Mode
    send: "#set_input_hdcp {input} {mode}\r\n"
    help: "Configured HDCP handshake for an input. Reject helps sources that force encryption (e.g. computers) on non-protected content."
    params:
      input:
        type: child_id
        child_type: input
        required: true
        label: Input
      mode:
        type: enum
        required: true
        label: Mode
        values:
          - { value: "0", label: "Reject HDCP" }
          - { value: "1", label: "HDCP 2.2 and below" }
          - { value: "2", label: "HDCP 1.4 and below" }

  set_output_hdcp:
    label: Set Output HDCP Mode
    send: "#set_output_hdcp {output} {mode}\r\n"
    params:
      output:
        type: child_id
        child_type: output
        required: true
        label: Output
      mode:
        type: enum
        required: true
        label: Mode
        values:
          - { value: "0", label: "Follow Input" }
          - { value: "1", label: "Always Encode" }

  set_edid_mode:
    label: Set Input EDID Mode
    send: "#set_edid_mode {input} {mode}\r\n"
    params:
      input:
        type: child_id
        child_type: input
        required: true
        label: Input
      mode:
        type: enum
        required: true
        label: EDID
        values:
          - { value: "0", label: "Internal 720p 2ch" }
          - { value: "1", label: "Internal 720p Multichannel" }
          - { value: "2", label: "Internal 1080p 2ch" }
          - { value: "3", label: "Internal 1080p Multichannel" }
          - { value: "4", label: "Internal 4K 2ch" }
          - { value: "5", label: "Internal 4K Multichannel" }
          - { value: "6", label: "External (Downstream Sink)" }
          - { value: "7", label: "Custom" }

  set_video_scaling:
    label: Set Output Video Scaling
    send: "#set_video_scaling {output} {state}\r\n"
    help: "EXT-UHD600A-44 only — outputs A and C downscale 2160p to 1080p, B and D upscale 1080p to 2160p."
    params:
      output:
        type: child_id
        child_type: output
        required: true
        label: Output
      state:
        type: enum
        required: true
        label: Scaling
        values:
          - { value: "0", label: "Disabled" }
          - { value: "1", label: "Enabled" }

  send_hpd:
    label: Send HPD Pulse to Input
    send: "#send_hpd {input}\r\n"
    help: "Pulse hot-plug detect toward the source — forces it to re-read the EDID and restart its output."
    params:
      input:
        type: child_id
        child_type: input
        required: true
        label: Input

  # ── System ──

  lock_front_panel:
    label: Lock Front Panel
    send: "#lock_matrix 1\r\n"
    help: "Locks the front panel and web interface (this driver keeps control)."

  unlock_front_panel:
    label: Unlock Front Panel
    send: "#lock_matrix 0\r\n"

  identify_on:
    label: Identify On (Show Me)
    send: "#set_showme 1\r\n"
    help: "Turns on the matrix's Show Me locate indication."

  identify_off:
    label: Identify Off
    send: "#set_showme 0\r\n"

  reboot:
    label: Reboot Matrix
    send: "#reboot\r\n"

  raw_command:
    label: Send Raw Command
    send: "{command}\r\n"
    help: "Send any CLI command from the manual, e.g. '#get_ipconfig' or '#help'."
    params:
      command:
        type: string
        required: true
        label: Command

actions:
  - id: route
    icon: route
  - id: route_all
    icon: copy
  - id: recall_preset
    icon: bookmark
  - id: mute_all_on
    icon: volume-x
  - id: identify_on
    icon: eye
  - id: reboot
    icon: power
    confirm: true

responses:

  # Input / output names — "IO_NAME 1 Bluray" / "IO_NAME D BIG_screen".
  # Digit vs letter disambiguates the two directions. These sit above
  # the routing rule so a name containing routing-like text can't
  # cross-fire.
  - match: 'IO_NAME\s+(\d{1,2})\s+(.+?)\s*$'
    child_set:
      - { type: input, id: "$1", state: { name: "$2" } }
  - match: 'IO_NAME\s+([A-H])\s+(.+?)\s*$'
    child_set:
      - { type: output, id: "$1", state: { name: "$2" } }

  # Analog audio — "VOL A 100", "MUTE A 0" (1 = muted).
  - match: 'VOL\s+([A-H])\s+(\d{1,3})\s*$'
    child_set:
      - { type: output, id: "$1", state: { volume: "$2" } }
  - match: 'MUTE\s+([A-H])\s+([01])\s*$'
    child_set:
      - { type: output, id: "$1", state: { mute: "$2" } }

  # Masking — "MASK A 1" (1 = masked).
  - match: 'MASK\s+([A-H])\s+([01])\s*$'
    child_set:
      - { type: output, id: "$1", state: { mask: "$2" } }

  # Audio mode — the get reply is "AUDIO_OUTPUT A V"; the set echo is
  # printed "AUDIO OUTPUT A F" in the manual, so accept both spellings.
  - match: 'AUDIO[_ ]OUTPUT\s+([A-H])\s+([FV])\s*$'
    child_set:
      - { type: output, id: "$1", state: { audio_mode: { group: 2, map: { F: "fixed", V: "variable" } } } }

  # Output sink status — "OUTPUT_HPD A H", "OUTPUT_RSENSE A L",
  # "OUTPUT_MODE A H" (H = HDMI, D = DVI), "OUTPUT_HDCP A 2".
  - match: 'OUTPUT_HPD\s+([A-H])\s+([LH])\s*$'
    child_set:
      - { type: output, id: "$1", state: { hpd: { group: 2, map: { H: "high", L: "low" } } } }
  - match: 'OUTPUT_RSENSE\s+([A-H])\s+([LH])\s*$'
    child_set:
      - { type: output, id: "$1", state: { rsense: { group: 2, map: { H: "high", L: "low" } } } }
  - match: 'OUTPUT_MODE\s+([A-H])\s+([DH])\s*$'
    child_set:
      - { type: output, id: "$1", state: { video_mode: { group: 2, map: { H: "hdmi", D: "dvi" } } } }
  - match: 'OUTPUT_HDCP\s+([A-H])\s+([12UF])\s*$'
    child_set:
      - { type: output, id: "$1", state: { hdcp_status: "$2" } }

  # Video scaling (44 only) — "VIDEO_SCALING A 1".
  - match: 'VIDEO_SCALING\s+([A-H])\s+([01])\s*$'
    child_set:
      - { type: output, id: "$1", state: { scaling: "$2" } }

  # Input status — "INPUT_SIGNAL 1 Y", "INPUT_MODE 1 H",
  # "INPUT_HDCP 1 2". Both manuals' #gets_input_hpd examples answer
  # with the INPUT_HDCP token, so the HPD rule accepts either token
  # with an L/H value (disjoint from the HDCP value sets).
  - match: 'INPUT_SIGNAL\s+(\d{1,2})\s+([YN])\s*$'
    child_set:
      - { type: input, id: "$1", state: { signal: { group: 2, map: { Y: "true", N: "false" } } } }
  - match: 'INPUT_H(?:PD|DCP)\s+(\d{1,2})\s+([LH])\s*$'
    child_set:
      - { type: input, id: "$1", state: { hpd: { group: 2, map: { H: "high", L: "low" } } } }
  - match: 'INPUT_HDCP\s+(\d{1,2})\s+([12UF])\s*$'
    child_set:
      - { type: input, id: "$1", state: { hdcp_status: "$2" } }
  - match: 'INPUT_MODE\s+(\d{1,2})\s+([DH])\s*$'
    child_set:
      - { type: input, id: "$1", state: { video_mode: { group: 2, map: { H: "hdmi", D: "dvi" } } } }

  # EDID mode — "EDID_MODE 1 0".
  - match: 'EDID_MODE\s+(\d{1,2})\s+([0-7])\s*$'
    child_set:
      - { type: input, id: "$1", state: { edid_mode: "$2" } }

  # Device-level values. "FIRMWARE VERSION IS 1.0",
  # "DEVICE DESCRIPTION IS EXT-UHD600A-44", "FEEDBACK 1" (get) /
  # "SET_FEEDBACK 1" (set echo), "IR_CHANNEL 1", brightness echoes.
  - match: 'FIRMWARE VERSION IS\s+(\S.*?)\s*$'
    set: { firmware_version: "$1" }
  - match: 'DEVICE DESCRIPTION IS\s+(\S.*?)\s*$'
    set: { device_desc: "$1" }
  - match: '(?:SET_)?FEEDBACK\s+([01])\s*$'
    set: { rs232_feedback: "$1" }
  - match: 'IR_CHANNEL\s+([1-4])\s*$'
    set: { ir_channel: "$1" }
  - match: 'LED_BRIGHTNESS\s+(\d{1,3})\s*$'
    set: { led_brightness: "$1" }
  - match: 'BUTTON_BRIGHTNESS\s+(\d{1,3})\s*$'
    set: { button_brightness: "$1" }
  - match: 'LCD_BRIGHTNESS\s+(\d{1,3})\s*$'
    set: { lcd_brightness: "$1" }

  # Routing — one rule covers every width: "R A 1" (single tie),
  # "R A X" (off), "R A 1 B 1 C 1" (multi-output route echo), and the
  # full dump "S A 1 B 2 C 3 D X" / 8-pair 88 form. Optional pairs that
  # didn't participate skip their child_set entry; X (and the 88
  # manual's bare 0) mean no input. Kept last — it's the loosest
  # pattern.
  - match: '\b[RS]\s+([A-H])\s+(\d+|X)(?:\s+([A-H])\s+(\d+|X))?(?:\s+([A-H])\s+(\d+|X))?(?:\s+([A-H])\s+(\d+|X))?(?:\s+([A-H])\s+(\d+|X))?(?:\s+([A-H])\s+(\d+|X))?(?:\s+([A-H])\s+(\d+|X))?(?:\s+([A-H])\s+(\d+|X))?\s*$'
    child_set:
      - { type: output, id: "$1", state: { input: { group: 2, map: { X: 0 } } } }
      - { type: output, id: "$3", state: { input: { group: 4, map: { X: 0 } } } }
      - { type: output, id: "$5", state: { input: { group: 6, map: { X: 0 } } } }
      - { type: output, id: "$7", state: { input: { group: 8, map: { X: 0 } } } }
      - { type: output, id: "$9", state: { input: { group: 10, map: { X: 0 } } } }
      - { type: output, id: "$11", state: { input: { group: 12, map: { X: 0 } } } }
      - { type: output, id: "$13", state: { input: { group: 14, map: { X: 0 } } } }
      - { type: output, id: "$15", state: { input: { group: 16, map: { X: 0 } } } }

on_connect:
  - "#get_firmware_version\r\n"
  - "#get_device_desc\r\n"
  - "s\r\n"

polling:
  queries:
    - "s\r\n"
    - { each_child: output, send: "#get_vol {child_id}\r\n" }
    - { each_child: output, send: "#get_mute {child_id}\r\n" }
    - { each_child: output, send: "#get_mask {child_id}\r\n" }
    - { each_child: output, send: "#get_audio_output {child_id}\r\n" }
    - { each_child: output, send: "#gets_output_hpd {child_id}\r\n" }
    - { each_child: output, send: "#gets_output_rsense {child_id}\r\n" }
    - { each_child: output, send: "#gets_output_mode {child_id}\r\n" }
    - { each_child: output, send: "#gets_output_hdcp {child_id}\r\n" }
    - { each_child: output, send: "#get_video_scaling {child_id}\r\n" }
    - { each_child: output, send: "#get_io_name {child_id}\r\n" }
    - { each_child: input, send: "#gets_input_signal {child_id}\r\n" }
    - { each_child: input, send: "#gets_input_hpd {child_id}\r\n" }
    - { each_child: input, send: "#gets_input_mode {child_id}\r\n" }
    - { each_child: input, send: "#gets_input_hdcp {child_id}\r\n" }
    - { each_child: input, send: "#get_edid_mode {child_id}\r\n" }
    - { each_child: input, send: "#get_io_name {child_id}\r\n" }
    - "#get_feedback\r\n"
    - "#get_ir_channel\r\n"
    - "#get_led_brightness\r\n"
    - "#get_button_brightness\r\n"
    - "#get_lcd_brightness\r\n"

# ── SIMULATOR DEFINITION ──
# Models an EXT-UHD600A-44 (matches the driver defaults): 4 inputs,
# outputs A-D with per-output volume/mute/mask/audio-mode/scaling and
# sink status, identity routing, three seeded presets, and the
# device-level settings. LCD brightness answers the 88-model error path
# (INVALID COMMAND) so the benign-error posture is exercised. Letters
# map to route_1..route_4 state keys so the Simulator UI matrix control
# works. One dispatcher script handler owns the whole grammar (matched
# script handlers never fall through).
simulator:
  push_state: false
  initial_state:
    outputs: "ABCD"
    inputs: 4
    route_1: 1
    route_2: 2
    route_3: 3
    route_4: 4
    vol_1: 100
    vol_2: 100
    vol_3: 100
    vol_4: 100
    mute_1: false
    mute_2: false
    mute_3: false
    mute_4: false
    mask_1: false
    mask_2: false
    mask_3: false
    mask_4: false
    audio_1: "V"
    audio_2: "V"
    audio_3: "V"
    audio_4: "V"
    out_hpd_1: "H"
    out_hpd_2: "H"
    out_hpd_3: "L"
    out_hpd_4: "H"
    out_rsense_1: "H"
    out_rsense_2: "H"
    out_rsense_3: "L"
    out_rsense_4: "H"
    out_mode_1: "H"
    out_mode_2: "H"
    out_mode_3: "H"
    out_mode_4: "H"
    out_hdcp_1: "2"
    out_hdcp_2: "1"
    out_hdcp_3: "U"
    out_hdcp_4: "2"
    scaling_1: false
    scaling_2: false
    scaling_3: false
    scaling_4: false
    out_name_1: "Output A"
    out_name_2: "Output B"
    out_name_3: "Output C"
    out_name_4: "Output D"
    in_signal_1: "Y"
    in_signal_2: "Y"
    in_signal_3: "N"
    in_signal_4: "Y"
    in_hpd_1: "H"
    in_hpd_2: "H"
    in_hpd_3: "L"
    in_hpd_4: "H"
    in_mode_1: "H"
    in_mode_2: "H"
    in_mode_3: "H"
    in_mode_4: "D"
    in_hdcp_1: "2"
    in_hdcp_2: "1"
    in_hdcp_3: "U"
    in_hdcp_4: "U"
    edid_1: 4
    edid_2: 4
    edid_3: 6
    edid_4: 0
    in_name_1: "Bluray"
    in_name_2: "PC"
    in_name_3: "Doc Cam"
    in_name_4: "Media Player"
    preset_1: "1,1,1,1"
    preset_2: "2,2,2,2"
    preset_3: "1,2,3,4"
    preset_name_1: "All Bluray"
    preset_name_2: "All PC"
    preset_name_3: "Default"
    feedback: true
    ir_channel: 1
    led_brightness: 60
    button_brightness: 60
    device_desc: "EXT-UHD600A-44"
    firmware: "1.0"
    showme: false
    locked: false

  delays:
    command_response: 0.05

  controls:
    - type: matrix
      label: Video Routing
      inputs: 4
      outputs: 4
      state_pattern: "route_{output}"
    - type: toggle
      key: mute_1
      label: Output A Mute
    - type: slider
      key: vol_1
      label: Output A Volume
      min: 0
      max: 100
    - type: toggle
      key: mask_1
      label: Output A Mask
    - type: toggle
      key: in_signal_1
      label: Input 1 Signal (Y/N)
    - type: toggle
      key: feedback
      label: RS-232 Feedback
    - type: indicator
      key: firmware
      label: Firmware

  command_handlers:

    # One dispatcher for the entire CLI grammar (a matched script
    # handler never falls through, so protocol-wide behavior lives in
    # one block). Letters map to numeric state suffixes (A=1...) so the
    # Simulator UI matrix control works. The body lives in _run() so
    # early returns are legal in the exec'd handler.
    - match: '.+'
      handler: |
        line = match.group(0).strip()
        low = line.lower()
        outs = str(state.get("outputs", "ABCD"))
        n_in = int(state.get("inputs", 4))

        def _idx(letter):
            pos = outs.upper().find(letter.upper())
            return pos + 1 if pos >= 0 else None

        def _letter(i):
            return outs.upper()[i - 1]

        def _b01(v):
            return "1" if v in (True, "true", 1, "1") else "0"

        def _run():
            # r <input|x> <out...|0>
            m = re.match(r'(?i)^r\s+(\d+|x)\s+(.+)$', low)
            if m:
                src = 0 if m.group(1) == "x" else int(m.group(1))
                if src > n_in:
                    respond("INVALID COMMAND\r\n")
                    return
                targets = m.group(2).split()
                if targets == ["0"]:
                    idxs = list(range(1, len(outs) + 1))
                else:
                    idxs = []
                    for t in targets:
                        i = _idx(t) if len(t) == 1 and t.isalpha() else None
                        if i is None:
                            respond("INVALID COMMAND\r\n")
                            return
                        idxs.append(i)
                for i in idxs:
                    state[f"route_{i}"] = src
                shown = "X" if src == 0 else str(src)
                respond("R " + " ".join(f"{_letter(i)} {shown}" for i in idxs) + "\r\n")
                return

            # s — full routing dump
            if low == "s":
                bits = []
                for i in range(1, len(outs) + 1):
                    v = int(state.get(f"route_{i}", 0))
                    bits.append(f"{_letter(i)} {'X' if v == 0 else v}")
                respond("S " + " ".join(bits) + "\r\n")
                return

            # p <n> — recall preset
            m = re.match(r'(?i)^p\s+(\d+)$', low)
            if m:
                slot = int(m.group(1))
                if 1 <= slot <= 16:
                    stored = state.get(f"preset_{slot}", "")
                    if stored:
                        vals = str(stored).split(",")
                        for i in range(1, min(len(vals), len(outs)) + 1):
                            state[f"route_{i}"] = int(vals[i - 1])
                    respond(f"P {slot}\r\n")
                else:
                    respond("INVALID COMMAND\r\n")
                return

            # #-commands
            m = re.match(r'^#(\w+)\s*(.*)$', low)
            if not m:
                respond("INVALID COMMAND\r\n")
                return
            cmd = m.group(1)
            rest = m.group(2).strip()
            args = rest.split() if rest else []

            def _out_arg(a):
                if len(a) == 1 and a.isalpha() and a.upper() in outs.upper():
                    return _idx(a)
                return None

            def _per_output(token, key, value=None, fmt=str, target=None):
                sel = target if target is not None else (args[0] if args else None)
                if sel is None:
                    respond("INVALID COMMAND\r\n")
                    return
                if sel == "0":
                    idxs = list(range(1, len(outs) + 1))
                else:
                    i = _out_arg(sel)
                    if i is None:
                        respond("INVALID COMMAND\r\n")
                        return
                    idxs = [i]
                lines = []
                for i in idxs:
                    if value is not None:
                        state[f"{key}_{i}"] = value
                    lines.append(f"{token} {_letter(i)} {fmt(state.get(f'{key}_{i}'))}")
                respond("\r\n".join(lines) + "\r\n")

            def _per_input(token, key, fmt=str):
                if not args:
                    respond("INVALID COMMAND\r\n")
                    return
                if args[0] == "0":
                    idxs = list(range(1, n_in + 1))
                else:
                    if not args[0].isdigit() or not 1 <= int(args[0]) <= n_in:
                        respond("INVALID COMMAND\r\n")
                        return
                    idxs = [int(args[0])]
                lines = [f"{token} {i} {fmt(state.get(f'{key}_{i}'))}" for i in idxs]
                respond("\r\n".join(lines) + "\r\n")

            def _upper(v):
                return str(v).upper()

            def _yn(v):
                return "Y" if v in (True, "true", "Y", 1) else "N"

            if cmd == "get_vol":
                _per_output("VOL", "vol")
            elif cmd == "set_vol" and len(args) >= 2:
                _per_output("VOL", "vol", target=args[0],
                            value=max(0, min(100, int(args[1]))))
            elif cmd == "get_mute":
                _per_output("MUTE", "mute", fmt=_b01)
            elif cmd == "set_mute" and len(args) >= 2:
                _per_output("MUTE", "mute", target=args[0],
                            value=(args[1] == "1"), fmt=_b01)
            elif cmd == "get_mask":
                _per_output("MASK", "mask", fmt=_b01)
            elif cmd == "set_mask" and len(args) >= 2:
                _per_output("MASK", "mask", target=args[0],
                            value=(args[1] == "1"), fmt=_b01)
            elif cmd == "get_audio_output":
                _per_output("AUDIO_OUTPUT", "audio", fmt=_upper)
            elif cmd == "set_audio_output" and len(args) >= 2:
                _per_output("AUDIO_OUTPUT", "audio", target=args[0],
                            value=args[1].upper(), fmt=_upper)
            elif cmd == "gets_output_hpd":
                _per_output("OUTPUT_HPD", "out_hpd")
            elif cmd == "gets_output_rsense":
                _per_output("OUTPUT_RSENSE", "out_rsense")
            elif cmd == "gets_output_mode":
                _per_output("OUTPUT_MODE", "out_mode")
            elif cmd == "gets_output_hdcp":
                _per_output("OUTPUT_HDCP", "out_hdcp")
            elif cmd == "get_video_scaling":
                _per_output("VIDEO_SCALING", "scaling", fmt=_b01)
            elif cmd == "set_video_scaling" and len(args) >= 2:
                _per_output("VIDEO_SCALING", "scaling", target=args[0],
                            value=(args[1] == "1"), fmt=_b01)
            elif cmd == "gets_input_signal":
                _per_input("INPUT_SIGNAL", "in_signal", fmt=_yn)
            elif cmd == "gets_input_hpd":
                _per_input("INPUT_HPD", "in_hpd")
            elif cmd == "gets_input_mode":
                _per_input("INPUT_MODE", "in_mode")
            elif cmd == "gets_input_hdcp":
                _per_input("INPUT_HDCP", "in_hdcp")
            elif cmd == "get_edid_mode":
                _per_input("EDID_MODE", "edid")
            elif cmd == "set_edid_mode" and len(args) >= 2:
                if (args[0].isdigit() and 1 <= int(args[0]) <= n_in
                        and args[1].isdigit() and 0 <= int(args[1]) <= 7):
                    state[f"edid_{args[0]}"] = int(args[1])
                    respond(f"EDID_MODE {args[0]} {args[1]}\r\n")
                else:
                    respond("INVALID COMMAND\r\n")
            elif cmd == "get_io_name" and args:
                i = _out_arg(args[0])
                if i is not None:
                    respond(f"IO_NAME {_letter(i)} {state.get(f'out_name_{i}')}\r\n")
                elif args[0].isdigit() and 1 <= int(args[0]) <= n_in:
                    respond(f"IO_NAME {args[0]} {state.get('in_name_' + args[0])}\r\n")
                else:
                    respond("INVALID COMMAND\r\n")
            elif cmd == "set_io_name" and len(args) >= 2:
                # take the name from the raw line to preserve case
                raw_parts = line.split(None, 2)
                name = raw_parts[2] if len(raw_parts) > 2 else ""
                if len(name) > 30:
                    respond("INVALID COMMAND\r\n")
                    return
                i = _out_arg(args[0])
                if i is not None:
                    state[f"out_name_{i}"] = name
                    respond(f"IO_NAME {_letter(i)} {name}\r\n")
                elif args[0].isdigit() and 1 <= int(args[0]) <= n_in:
                    state[f"in_name_{args[0]}"] = name
                    respond(f"IO_NAME {args[0]} {name}\r\n")
                else:
                    respond("INVALID COMMAND\r\n")
            elif cmd == "get_preset_name" and args and args[0].isdigit():
                slot = int(args[0])
                if 1 <= slot <= 16:
                    respond(f"PRESET_NAME {slot} {state.get(f'preset_name_{slot}', '')}\r\n")
                else:
                    respond("INVALID COMMAND\r\n")
            elif cmd == "set_preset_name" and len(args) >= 2 and args[0].isdigit():
                raw_parts = line.split(None, 2)
                slot = int(args[0])
                if 1 <= slot <= 16 and len(raw_parts) > 2:
                    state[f"preset_name_{slot}"] = raw_parts[2]
                    respond(f"PRESET_NAME {slot} {raw_parts[2]}\r\n")
                else:
                    respond("INVALID COMMAND\r\n")
            elif cmd == "set_input_hdcp" and len(args) >= 2:
                # Mode echo shares the INPUT_HDCP token with the live
                # status (the documented collision).
                respond(f"INPUT_HDCP {args[0]} {args[1]}\r\n")
            elif cmd == "get_input_hdcp":
                _per_input("INPUT_HDCP", "in_hdcp_mode",
                           fmt=lambda v: "1" if v is None else str(v))
            elif cmd == "set_output_hdcp" and len(args) >= 2:
                i = _out_arg(args[0])
                if i is not None:
                    respond(f"OUTPUT_HDCP {_letter(i)} {args[1]}\r\n")
                else:
                    respond("INVALID COMMAND\r\n")
            elif cmd == "get_output_hdcp":
                _per_output("OUTPUT_HDCP", "out_hdcp_mode",
                            fmt=lambda v: "0" if v is None else str(v))
            elif cmd == "send_hpd":
                respond("HPD SENT\r\n")
            elif cmd == "lock_matrix" and args:
                state["locked"] = args[0] == "1"
                respond(f"LOCK_MATRIX {args[0]}\r\n")
            elif cmd == "set_showme" and args:
                state["showme"] = args[0] == "1"
                respond(f"SET_SHOWME {args[0]}\r\n")
            elif cmd == "get_showme":
                respond(f"SHOWME {_b01(state.get('showme'))}\r\n")
            elif cmd == "get_feedback":
                respond(f"FEEDBACK {_b01(state.get('feedback'))}\r\n")
            elif cmd == "set_feedback" and args:
                state["feedback"] = args[0] == "1"
                respond(f"SET_FEEDBACK {args[0]}\r\n")
            elif cmd == "get_ir_channel":
                respond(f"IR_CHANNEL {state.get('ir_channel')}\r\n")
            elif cmd == "set_ir_channel" and args:
                state["ir_channel"] = int(args[0])
                respond(f"IR_CHANNEL {args[0]}\r\n")
            elif cmd == "get_led_brightness":
                respond(f"LED_BRIGHTNESS {state.get('led_brightness')}\r\n")
            elif cmd == "set_led_brightness" and args:
                state["led_brightness"] = int(args[0])
                respond(f"LED_BRIGHTNESS {args[0]}\r\n")
            elif cmd == "get_button_brightness":
                respond(f"BUTTON_BRIGHTNESS {state.get('button_brightness')}\r\n")
            elif cmd == "set_button_brightness" and args:
                state["button_brightness"] = int(args[0])
                respond(f"BUTTON_BRIGHTNESS {args[0]}\r\n")
            elif cmd in ("get_lcd_brightness", "set_lcd_brightness"):
                # This sim models a 44 — no LCD; exercises the driver's
                # benign-error posture for the other model's settings.
                respond("INVALID COMMAND\r\n")
            elif cmd == "get_device_desc":
                respond(f"DEVICE DESCRIPTION IS {state.get('device_desc')}\r\n")
            elif cmd == "set_device_desc":
                raw_parts = line.split(None, 1)
                desc = raw_parts[1] if len(raw_parts) > 1 else ""
                state["device_desc"] = desc
                respond(f"DEVICE DESCRIPTION IS {desc}\r\n")
            elif cmd == "get_firmware_version":
                respond(f"FIRMWARE VERSION IS {state.get('firmware')}\r\n")
            elif cmd == "reboot":
                respond("UNIT WILL REBOOT SHORTLY\r\n")
            elif cmd == "help":
                respond("#help\r\n")
            else:
                respond("INVALID COMMAND\r\n")

        _run()
